ents before it are greater than it (count_g) and how many are less than it (count_l). The function returns True if count_g == count_l. \n\nBut wait, that\'s not exactly the case. Because for the integer x in the list, for each occurrence, we need to count how many elements before it are greater (count_g) and how many are less (count_l). The function returns True if for all occurrences of x, these counts are equal? Or if the total across all occurrences?\n\nWait the function is named solve and takes a list of integers and a single integer. So the list may contain multiple instances of that integer. The problem\'s wording is ambiguous. But given the example I thought of earlier, with list [3,1,2] and x=2, the counts for x would be count_g=1, count_l=1. So the function returns True. If the list is [2,3,1,2], x=2, then for the first occurrence of 2 (index 0), no elements before it, counts are 0. For the second occurrence (index 3), elements before it are [2,3,1]. Elements greater than 2 are 3 (count_g=1), elements less than 2 are 1 (count_l=1). So for each occurrence, the total counts would be 0 (first 2) and 1 & 1 (second 2). But the problem\'s wording isn\'t clear on whether to consider all occurrences or just the total. \n\nBut the problem says "the number of inversions where the integer is less than an element earlier in the list is the same as those that are greater". So, perhaps the function is counting all such instances across the entire list. For example, for each occurrence of the integer x in the list, count the number of elements before it that are greater than x (summing all these counts to get total_greater), and the number of elements before it that are less than x (total_less). Then return whether total_greater == total_less.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is not considering the occurrences of x in the list, but the given integer is the target. For example, the list may not contain the integer, but the function should check how many elements in the 