 to sqrt(n):\n\nWhile i divides n:\n\n- add i to sum.\n\n- divide n by i.\n\nOnce that\'s done, if n > 1, add n to sum.\n\nWait, but in the loop, after adding i once, we need to divide n by i until it\'s no longer divisible. So for example, for n=12, when i=2, we divide by 2 twice, adding 2 once. Then check i=3, which divides 3, add 3, and divide by 3 once. Then n becomes 1, so the loop ends.\n\nSo the code steps would be:\n\ndef Sum(n):\n\n    sum_primes = 0\n\n    i = 2\n\n    while i * i <= n:\n\n        while n % i == 0:\n\n            sum_primes += i\n\n            n = n // i\n\n        i += 1\n\n    if n > 1:\n\n        sum_primes += n\n\n    return sum_primes\n\nWait, but what if n is 1? Then the function returns 0. Because 1 has no prime divisors. Let\'s test that. For n=1, the code would loop i from 2, but since i*i is 4 > 1, the loop doesn\'t run. Then check if n>1: no. So returns 0. Correct.\n\nAnother test case: n=2. The loop runs i=2, i*i=4 > 2. So loop doesn\'t run. Then n is 2>1, add 2. Sum is 2. Correct.\n\nn=3: same logic, sum is 3.\n\nn=4: i starts at 2. 2 divides 4, add 2, divide by 2 to get 2. Then loop again, 2 divides 2, add 2 again, divide by 2 to get 1. Then i increases to 3, but 3*3 >1. So loop ends. Then n is 1, so no addition. Total sum is 2+2=4. Which is correct because prime divisors of 4 are 2 (only once, but since it\'s a divisor, even though it\'s squared, we count it once). Wait wait, the problem says "sum of all prime divisors of n". Wait, does that mean the sum of distinct prime divisors, or the sum of all prime factors (with multiplicity)?\n\nWait the problem says "sum of all prime divisors of n". Let me check the example. For example, if n is 4, the prime divisors are 2. So the sum would be 2. But according to the code above, the code would add 2 twice. So that\'s a problem.\n\nWait, the problem statement says "sum of all prime divisors of n". Let me check the actual definition. Prime divisors are the primes that divide n. So for