omplete the square.\n\nStarting with y = ax² + bx + c. Factor out a from the first two terms:\n\ny = a(x² + (b/a)x) + c.\n\nComplete the square inside the parentheses. The coefficient of x is b/a. Take half of that, square it: (b/(2a))² = b²/(4a²). So add and subtract that inside:\n\ny = a[ (x² + (b/a)x + b²/(4a²)) - b²/(4a²) ] + c\n= a(x + b/(2a))² - a*(b²/(4a²)) + c\n= a(x + b/(2a))² - b²/(4a) + c.\n\nSo the vertex is at (-b/(2a), c - b²/(4a)). \n\nTherefore, k = c - (b²)/(4a). Then the directrix is y = k - 1/(4a) = c - (b²)/(4a) - 1/(4a) = c - (b² + 1)/(4a). Wait, that seems right?\n\nWait, no. Wait the vertex form is y = a(x - h)^2 + k, where h is -b/(2a) and k is c - b²/(4a). Then the directrix is y = k - 1/(4a). So substituting k, the directrix is [c - b²/(4a)] - 1/(4a) = c - (b² + 1)/(4a). \n\nWait, but that would be the case if the standard form is y = a(x - h)^2 + k. But when we derived it, the directrix formula for that form is y = k - 1/(4a). So that seems correct. \n\nAlternatively, I can look up the formula for the directrix of a parabola given in standard form. Let me think. For the general quadratic equation y = ax² + bx + c, the directrix is given by y = (1 - (b² + 4ac)) / (4a). Wait, maybe I should derive it again to be sure.\n\nLet me rederive:\n\nThe vertex is at (h, k) where h = -b/(2a) and k = c - (b²)/(4a).\n\nThe directrix is located at a distance of 1/(4a) from the vertex, but in the opposite direction of the parabola's opening. Since the parabola opens upwards if a is positive, the directrix is below the vertex by 1/(4a). So the y-coordinate of the directrix is k - 1/(4a). \n\nSubstituting k:\n\ndirectrix = [c - (b²)/(4a)] - 1/(4a) = c - (b² + 1)/(4a).\n\nSo the formula is y = c - (b² + 1)/(4a).\n\nWait, but that seems a bit off. Let me check with an example. Let's take the parabola y = x². Here, a=1, b=0, c=0. The vertex is at (0, 0). The directrix should be y = -1/(4*1) = -0.25. According to the formula c - (b² + 1)/(4a) = 0 - (0 +1)/4 = -