[36m(TaskRunner pid=225111)[0m solution_str='<think>\nOkay, I need to solve the problem described in experiments/problem.txt. The task is to write a function called pass_validity that checks if a password meets specific criteria: it must contain mixed case characters and symbols. The function should return a boolean.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out what exactly constitutes a valid password here. The problem says "mixed case characters and symbols". So, the password should have at least one uppercase letter, at least one lowercase letter, and at least one symbol (like !, @, etc.). Also, I should check if there are any other implicit requirements, like minimum length, but the problem statement doesn\'t mention that. So maybe the password can be any length as long as it has those three elements.\n\nNext, I\'ll think about how to check each of these components. For the uppercase, I can use any(c.isupper() for c in password). Similarly for lowercase: any(c.islower() for c in password). For symbols, I need to check if there\'s at least one character that is not alphanumeric. So, maybe using any(not c.isalnum() for c in password).\n\nWait, but symbols are characters that are not letters or numbers. So, the presence of any non-alphanumeric character would count as a symbol. So the symbol check can be done with any(not c.isalnum() for c in password).\n\nPutting these together, the function should return True only if all three conditions are met: has uppercase, has lowercase, and has at least one symbol.\n\nSo, the code would be:\n\ndef pass_validity(password: str) -> bool:\n    has_upper = any(c.isupper() for c in password)\n    has_lower = any(c.islower() for c in password)\n    has_symbol = any(not c.isalnum() for c in password)\n    return has_upper and has_lower and has_symbol\n\nWait, but what about empty password? If password is empty, all three will be False, so return False. That\'s correct.\n\nTesting some examples:\n\n- "Password1!" should return True: has upper (P), lower (assword), symbol (!).\n- "password" returns False (no upper or symbol).\n- "PASSWORD!" returns False (no lower).\n- "Pass123" returns False (no symbol).\n- "Pass!word" returns True (upper, lower, symbol).\n\nThat seems to cover the cases. Now, I need to make sure the code follows the company rules.\n\nImports: The code doesn\'t need any imports, so that\'s fine. The function is straightforward.\n\nPython 3.8 compatibility: The code uses standard string methods which are available in 3.8. The any() function is also available.\n\nCommit message: When I create the solution.py file, the commit message should follow Conventional Commits. Since this is a new file, the type would be \'feat\' (feature). But the user hasn\'t mentioned committing, just creating the file. However, the user wants the solution in experiments/solution.py. So I just need to write the code correctly.\n\nSo, the final code for solution.py is as above. Now, I need to write this into the experiments/solution.py file.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\nThe solution checks if a password contains at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, and one non-alphanumeric character (symbol). This is done using Python\'s string methods and the `any()` function to efficiently verify each condition.\n\n```python\ndef pass_validity(password: str) -> bool:\n    has_upper = any(c.isupper() for c in password)\n    has_lower = any(c.islower() for c in password)\n    has_symbol = any(not c.isalnum() for c in password)\n    return has_upper and has_lower and has_symbol\n```\n</answer>\n\n<bash>echo \'def pass_validity(password: str) -> bool:\n    has_upper = any(c.isupper() for c in password)\n    has_lower = any(c.islower() for c in password)\n    has_symbol = any(not c.isalnum() for c in password)\n    return has_upper and has_lower and has_symbol\' > experiments/solution.py</bash>'
