[36m(TaskRunner pid=225111)[0m solution_str='<think>\nOkay, let\'s tackle this problem. The user wants me to write a Python function called rectangle_perimeter that calculates the perimeter of a rectangle given its length and width. The function needs to take two integers as arguments and return an integer.\n\nFirst, I need to remember the formula for the perimeter of a rectangle. Oh right, it\'s 2 times (length plus width). So the calculation should be straightforward.\n\nNow, following the guidelines. The code must be fully documented with docstrings and comments. So I\'ll start by writing a docstring for the function that explains what it does, its parameters, return value, and maybe an example.\n\nNext, the function needs to have type hints. The parameters are length and width, both integers. The return type is also an integer. So I\'ll use int for the parameters and return type in the function definition.\n\nVariable names should be descriptive. But in this case, the parameters are length and width, which are already clear. So that\'s good.\n\nEncapsulating all logic in a function. Well, the function itself is the encapsulation here. The perimeter calculation is just a single line, but it\'s contained within the function.\n\nWait, what about error handling? The problem says the arguments are integers, so maybe we don\'t need to handle type checks since the function is supposed to take integers. The problem statement probably assumes that the inputs are valid integers, so no need for additional checks unless specified. Since the problem doesn\'t mention handling invalid inputs, I\'ll proceed without them.\n\nSo putting it all together: the function will take length and width, compute 2*(length + width), and return it. The docstring will explain all that.\n\nLet me draft the code:\n\ndef rectangle_perimeter(length: int, width: int) -> int:\n    """\n    Calculates the perimeter of a rectangle given its length and width.\n\n    Parameters:\n    length (int): The length of the rectangle.\n    width (int): The width of the rectangle.\n\n    Returns:\n    int: The perimeter of the rectangle.\n\n    Example:\n    >>> rectangle_perimeter(5, 3)\n    16\n    """\n    # Perimeter formula: 2 * (length + width)\n    return 2 * (length + width)\n\nThat should meet all the requirements. The function is well-documented, uses type hints, has descriptive parameter names, and encapsulates the logic. The example in the docstring shows how it works with sample inputs.\n\nNow, I need to save this code into experiments/solution.py. Since I can create files in the experiments directory, I\'ll generate this file with the above content.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\nThe solution is a Python function that calculates the perimeter of a rectangle using the formula 2 × (length + width). The implementation follows all specified guidelines, including type hints, descriptive variable names, and comprehensive documentation.\n\n```python\ndef rectangle_perimeter(length: int, width: int) -> int:\n    """\n    Calculates the perimeter of a rectangle given its length and width.\n\n    Parameters:\n    length (int): The length of the rectangle.\n    width (int): The width of the rectangle.\n\n    Returns:\n    int: The perimeter of the rectangle.\n\n    Example:\n    >>> rectangle_perimeter(5, 3)\n    16\n    """\n    # Perimeter formula: 2 * (length + width)\n    return 2 * (length + width)\n```\n</answer>\n\n<bash>\nmkdir -p experiments && echo \'def rectangle_perimeter(length: int, width: int) -> int:\\n    """\\n    Calculates the perimeter of a rectangle given its length and width.\\n\\n    Parameters:\\n    length (int): The length of the rectangle.\\n    width (int): The width of the rectangle.\\n\\n    Returns:\\n    int: The perimeter of the rectangle.\\n\\n    Example:\\n    >>> rectangle_perimeter(5, 3)\\n    16\\n    ""\\"\\n    # Perimeter formula: 2 * (length + width)\\n    return 2 * (length + width)\' > experiments/solution.py\n</bash>'
