is [1, 2]. The Bell number for 1 is 1, which is the first element.\n\nRow 2 starts with 2 (last element of row 1). Then next element is 2 (row 1\'s first element) + 2 (row 2\'s first element) = 4? Wait, that doesn\'t match the earlier example. Wait maybe I got the indices wrong.\n\nWait the Bell triangle construction: Each row starts with the last element of the previous row. Then, each subsequent element is the sum of the previous element in the same row and the element from the previous row\'s same position.\n\nLet me check an example. Let\'s build the triangle step by step.\n\nRow 0: [1] → Bell number B0 = 1.\n\nRow 1: starts with B0 (1). Then the next element is row1[0] + row0[0] = 1 + 1 = 2. So row1 is [1, 2]. Bell number B1 is 1.\n\nRow 2: starts with 2 (last element of row 1). Next element is row2[0] + row1[1] (since row1 has elements 0 and 1). Wait row1 is [1,2], so row2\'s second element is row2[0] (which is 2) + row1[1] (which is 2) → 4? But earlier I thought row2 should be [2, 3, 5], which would give B2=2. But according to the Bell triangle\'s standard construction, maybe I\'m misunderstanding.\n\nWait perhaps I\'m getting confused with the Bell triangle method. Let me double-check the correct way to build the Bell triangle.\n\nThe Bell triangle is constructed as follows:\n\n- The first row starts with B0 = 1.\n- Each subsequent row starts with the last element of the previous row.\n- Each element after the first in a row is the sum of the previous element in the same row and the element from the previous row\'s same position.\n\nSo, let\'s build the triangle correctly.\n\nRow 0: [1] → B0=1\nRow 1: [1 (from previous row\'s last element), 1+1=2] → B1=1\nRow 2: [2 (from previous row\'s last element), 2 (current row\'s previous element) + 1 (previous row\'s 0th element) = 3, then 3 + 2 (previous row\'s 1st element) = 5] → row 2 is [2,3,5], B2=2\nRow 3: [5 (from previous row\'s last element), 5+2=7, 7+3=10, 10+5=15] → row3 is [5,7,10,15], B3=5.\n\nYes, that\'s correct. So for the Bell number Bn, it\'s the first element of row n.\n\nSo to compute Bn, we need to build up the triangle until row n, and take the first element of that row.\n\nSo, for n=0, return 1. For n=1, return 1. For n=2, return 2, etc.\n\nNow, the function needs to compute this. Let\'s think about the algorithm.\n\nInitialize a list of lists. Start with the first row as [1]. Then for each row from 1 to n:\n\n- The first element is the last element of the previous row.\n- Then, for each subsequent element in the row, it\'s the sum of the previous element in the current row and the element from the previous row at the same index.\n\nWait, for each row i (starting from 0), the row has i+1 elements. For example, row 0 has 1 element, row 1 has 2 elements, row 2 has 3 elements, etc.\n\nSo for row i (starting from 0), the row starts with the last element of row i-1. Then, for each j in 1 to i, the element is row[i][j-1] + row[i-1][j-1].\n\nSo for each row, we can build it step by step.\n\nNow, implementing this in code.\n\nLet me think about the code steps:\n\ndef bell_Number(n: int) -> int:\n    # Check input type\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError("Input must be an integer")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError("Input must be a non-negative integer")\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    # Initialize triangle\n    triangle = [[1]]  # row 0\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        # Start with the last element of previous row\n        current_row = [triangle[i-1][-1]]\n        for j in range(1, i+1):\n            # current_row[j] = current_row[j-1] + triangle[i-1][j-1]\n            # but since triangle[i-1] has length i elements (since row i-1 is for index i-1 starting from 0)\n            # Wait, triangle[i-1] is row i-1, which has i elements. For example, row 0 has 1 element, row 1 has 2, etc.\n            # So for j in 1 to i (since current row is row i, which has i+1 elements. Wait, i starts from 1 to n. So for row i (which is the ith row, starting from 0?), no, maybe I need to reindex.\n\nWait, perhaps the loop should run from 1 to n, but the rows are built from 1 to n. Let me think again.\n\nThe Bell number Bn is the first element of row n in the Bell triangle. So for n=0, it\'s row 0. For n=1, row 1. So the triangle has rows 0 to n.\n\nSo, if the function is given n, we need to generate rows 0 up to n. Then, the first element of row n is the answer.\n\nSo for example, for n=2, generate rows 0, 1, 2. The first element of row 2 is 2, which is B2.\n\nSo in the code:\n\nInitialize triangle as [[1]] (row 0).\n\nThen, for i in 1 to n (inclusive):\n\ncurrent_row starts with the last element of the previous row (triangle[i-1][-1]).\n\nThen, for j from 1 to i (since the row i has i+1 elements, but the index starts from 0? Wait, for row i, the length is i+1 elements? Let me see:\n\nrow 0 has 1 element (i=0), row 1 has 2 elements (i=1), row 2 has 3 elements (i=2). So for row i, the number of elements is i+1. So when building row i, we need to generate i+1 elements. But the loop for building the row is from j=1 to j=i, adding elements to current_row.\n\nWait, current_row starts with the first element (the last of previous row). Then, for j from 1 to i, we add elements. Because the row i has i+1 elements, and j ranges from 0 to i (indices 0 to i). So the first element is index 0, and the loop for j runs from 1 to i, adding i elements, total of i+1 elements.\n\nSo for each row i (starting from 1 to n), we generate a row of length i+1.\n\nSo in the code:\n\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    current_row = [triangle[i-1][-1]]  # first element\n    for j in range(1, i + 1):\n        # the previous element in current_row is at j-1, and triangle[i-1][j-1]\n        current_row.append(current_row[j-1] + triangle[i-1][j-1])\n    triangle.append(current_row)\n\nWait, but for j in 1 to i (inclusive), the loop would run i times, adding i elements. Since the current_row starts with 1 element, after the loop, it would have 1 + i elements. For example, when i=1, the loop runs j=1 once, adding 1 element. So current_row has 2 elements (correct for row 1).\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n\nOnce all rows are built up to row n, the Bell number is triangle[n][0].\n\nSo the code would proceed as:\n\nif n == 0:\n    return 1\ntriangle = [[1]]\nfor i in range(1, n+1):\n    current_row = [triangle[i-1][-1]]\n    for j in range(1, i + 1):\n        current_row.append(current_row[j-1] + triangle[i-1][j-1])\n    triangle.append(current_row)\nreturn triangle[n][0]\n\nNow, let\'s test this logic with some examples.\n\nFor n=0: returns 1 → correct.\n\nFor n=1: triangle has row 0 [1], then i=1.\n\ncurrent_row starts with [1] (from row 0\'s last element). Then j runs from 1 to 1 (i=1, j=1).\n\ncurrent_row.append(current_row[0] + triangle[0][0] → 1 + 1 = 2 → current_row becomes [1, 2]. triangle is [[1], [1,2]]. Bell number is triangle[1][0] = 1 → correct.\n\nFor n=2: i runs up to 2.\n\ni=1: as above.\n\ni=2: current_row starts with [2] (from row 1\'s last element, 2). Then j runs from 1 to 2 (i=2):\n\nj=1: current_row[0] (2) + triangle[1][0] (1) → 3 → current_row is [2,3].\n\nj=2: current_row[1] (3) + triangle[1][1] (2) → 5 → current_row [2,3,5]. So triangle now has row 2 as [2,3,5]. Bell number is 2.\n\nWhich is correct.\n\nAnother test: n=3. After building up to row 3.\n\nThe triangle would be:\n\nrow 0: [1]\n\nrow 1: [1, 2]\n\nrow 2: [2,3,5]\n\nrow 3: starts with 5 (from row 2\'s last element). Then j from 1 to 3:\n\nj=1: current_row[0] (5) + triangle[2][0] (2) → 7 → current_row [5,7]\n\nj=2: current_row[1] (7) + triangle[2][1] (3) → 10 → current_row [5,7,10]\n\nj=3: current_row[2] (10) + triangle[2][2] (5) → 15 → current_row [5,7,10,15]\n\nSo triangle[3][0] is 5 → B3=5. Correct.\n\nSo this algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow, implementing this in Python with the required type hints and input validation.\n\nThe function must have:\n\n- Type hints for parameters and return type.\n\n- Use isinstance() to check