ement open_count.\n\nBut wait, this would count the number of unmatched closing brackets. However, each swap would fix two brackets. For example, each swap would fix one unmatched closing and one unmatched opening. So the total number of swaps would be the number of unmatched closing brackets (since each swap fixes one of them).\n\nWait, maybe the correct approach is that the number of swaps required is equal to the number of unmatched closing brackets. Because each swap would take one closing bracket that\'s unmatched and swap it with an opening bracket that\'s also unmatched. So the number of swaps is the count of these mismatches.\n\nWait, maybe not. Let me think again.\n\nConsider the string "] [". The correct answer is 1 swap. Let\'s apply the algorithm:\n\nInitial open_count = 0, swap_count = 0.\n\nFirst character is \']\', open_count is 0. So swap_count increments by 1. open_count remains 0.\n\nSecond character is \'[\', increment open_count to 1.\n\nAt the end, open_count is 1. So the number of swaps is 1. Which is correct.\n\nAnother example: "]] [[". Let\'s process:\n\nFirst \']\' → swap_count becomes 1.\n\nSecond \']\' → swap_count becomes 2.\n\nThird \'[\' → open_count 1.\n\nFourth \'[\' → open_count 2.\n\nTotal swaps: 2. Which is correct.\n\nAnother example: "] [] [". Let\'s see:\n\nFirst \']\' → swap_count 1.\n\nThen \'[\', open_count 1.\n\nThen \']\' → open_count 0.\n\nThen \'[\', open_count 1.\n\nSo swap_count is 1. But in this case, the string is "] [ ] [", which would need one swap to make "[ ] [ ]", which is balanced. So that\'s correct.\n\nSo the algorithm is: count the number of times a closing bracket is encountered when open_count is zero. That count is the number of swaps needed.\n\nTherefore, the code would be:\n\ndef swap_count(s):\n    open_count = 0\n    swap_count = 0\n    for char in s:\n        if char == \'[\':\n            open_count += 1\n        elif char == \']\':\n            if open_count == 0:\n                swap_count += 1\n