n using built-ins.\n\nWait, the problem says the function should return the larger value. So both approaches are valid. But given that the company's rules are about code quality and structure, perhaps the solution should be as simple as possible. So using the built-in max function would be the most efficient and concise way, fitting the rules of minimal code.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user expects the solution to be written with an if-else statement. For example:\n\ndef max_of_two(a, b):\n    if a > b:\n        return a\n    else:\n        return b\n\nThis is a straightforward approach, no helper functions, one function, linear code. This would comply with all the given rules.\n\nBut the company's rules don't mention avoiding built-in functions. So either approach is acceptable. Which one is better? Since the problem is simple, using the built-in function is more efficient and less code. However, perhaps the user expects the explicit logic.\n\nBut the problem statement says that the function should return the maximum. So using max(a, b) would be correct. However, perhaps the company's guidelines prefer explicit logic over built-in functions. But there's no indication of that in the given rules. The rules are about structure, not about using built-ins.\n\nIn that case, both methods are acceptable. But to ensure the code is as simple as possible, using the built-in function would be better. However, maybe the user expects the solution to not use built-in functions, but that's not stated here.\n\nGiven that the user hasn't specified any restrictions on using built-in functions, I can use the max function. But to make sure, let's go with the if-else approach. It's more explicit and ensures that the code is self-contained without relying on built-in functions. That way, it's clear and meets the company's rules of being a linear script with minimal functions.\n\nSo the solution would be a single function max_of_two with an if-else statement comparing the two inputs and 