where the second has a 1, and another position where the first has a 1 and the second has a 0. Those two can be swapped, which will fix both. So the total number of swaps is the number of differing positions divided by 2.\n\nBut how about when there are multiple 0s and 1s in the differing positions. For example, if the first string is "0101" and the second is "1010", the differing positions are all four. So two swaps needed. Which is 4/2 = 2.\n\nSo the logic seems to hold. So the code would be:\n\nCheck if the lengths are same. If not, return "Not Possible".\n\nThen count the number of differing characters. If that is odd, return "Not Possible".\n\nElse, return (count // 2).\n\nWait, but how do I count the differing characters? For each index, compare the characters of the two strings.\n\nSo, in Python, for the two strings a and b, the count can be sum(1 for i in range(len(a)) if a[i] != b[i]).\n\nSo the steps are:\n\n- Check if len(a) != len(b): return "Not Possible".\n\n- Compute diff_count = sum( ... as above).\n\n- if diff_count % 2 != 0: return "Not Possible".\n\n- else: return diff_count // 2.\n\nBut wait, what about cases where the number of 0s and 1s in the differing positions isn\'t balanced? For example, if the first string has two 0s and the second has two 1s. But that\'s not possible because each differing position is a 0 in one and 1 in the other. So the total number of 0s in the differing positions of the first string is equal to the number of 1s in the second string\'s corresponding positions. So the count must be even because each differing position contributes to a 0 and a 1. Therefore, the count of differing positions is even if and only if the number of 0s and 1s in those positions are equal, which is required for the swaps.\n\nSo the code seems correct.\n\nNow, considering the problem statement, the function is named min_Swaps and takes two binary strings. So the code would be:\n\ndef min_Swaps(a, b):\n    if len(a) != len(b):\n        return "No