ime divisors are the same as for 6, since divisors are considered in absolute value. Wait, the problem says "prime divisors of n". But primes are positive. So, for example, if n is negative, the prime divisors are the same as for the absolute value. So the function should take absolute value of n.\n\nWait, the problem says "prime divisors of n". But primes are positive. So, for example, -6\'s prime divisors are 2 and 3. So the code should process n as absolute value.\n\nAh! That\'s a point I missed. The current code would process n as is. So if n is negative, the code would process it as negative. But in the loop, when i starts at 2, and n is negative, the modulo operation might behave differently. For example, n=-6, i=2. n % i is 0? Let\'s see.\n\nIn Python, -6 % 2 is 0. Because -6 divided by 2 is -3 with remainder 0. So yes, the code would work even for negative n. Because the modulo operation would still find the divisors.\n\nBut let\'s test with n=-6. The code would process n as -6. The first i=2. n %i is 0. So add 2 to sum. Then divide n by 2 until it\'s no longer divisible. So n becomes -6 / 2 = -3. Then check again, -3 %2 is -1, which is not zero. So the loop ends. Then i increments. Then i=3. i*i=9, which is larger than abs(n) (3). So loop ends. Then check if n>1? n is -3, which is not >1. So no addition. So sum is 2. But the prime divisors of -6 are 2 and 3. So the code returns 2, but the correct sum is 5. So there\'s a problem here.\n\nAh, right. Because the code is processing n as a negative number. So the code as written would not handle negative numbers correctly. Thus, we need to take absolute value of n.\n\nSo the code should process the absolute value of n. Because prime divisors are for the absolute value. So the code should first take n = abs(n).\n\nSo adjust the code:\n\nInside the function:\n\nn = abs(n)\n\nBut wait, after that, the code proceeds. Let me see:\n\ndef Sum(n: int) -> int:\n\n    assert isinstance(n, int), "Input must be an integer."