in experiments/solution.py. So I\'ll write the function there. The test should be in a separate file, perhaps experiments/test_solution.py. Let me structure that.\n\nFor the test, using Python\'s unittest framework. The test case would check various scenarios:\n\n- A string with spaces, like "hello world" becomes "helloworld".\n- A string with no spaces, returns the same.\n- A string with leading and trailing spaces, like "  test  " becomes "test".\n- A string with multiple spaces between words, like "a   b" becomes "ab".\n\nSo the test code would be:\n\nimport unittest\nfrom experiments.solution import remove_spaces\n\nclass TestRemoveSpaces(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_remove_spaces(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("hello world"), "helloworld")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("  leading and trailing  "), "leadingandtrailing")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("no spaces here"), "no spaces here")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("multiple   spaces"), "multiplespaces")\n\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    unittest.main()\n\nBut need to make sure the imports are alphabetized. In the test file, the imports would be:\n\nimport unittest\nfrom experiments.solution import remove_spaces\n\nWhich is already in order.\n\nSo putting it all together. The solution file has the function, the test file has the tests. I need to create both files in the experiments directory.\n\nNow, the user is using a bash agent, so I need to generate the necessary commands to create these files. The solution.py and test_solution.py files. Since I can write to the experiments directory, I\'ll create them.\n\nSo the bash command would be to echo the contents into those files. But I need to make sure that the files are created correctly. Also, ensuring that the code follows all the rules.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\nThe solution involves creating a function `remove_spaces` that removes all spaces from a string and writing corresponding unit tests. Here\'s the implementation:\n\n```python\n# experiments/solution.py\ndef remove_spaces(s):\n    return s.replace(\' \', \'\')\n```\n\n```python\n# experiments/test_solution.py\nimport unittest\nfrom experiments.solution import remove_spaces\n\nclass TestRemoveSpaces(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_remove_spaces(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("hello world"), "helloworld")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("  leading and trailing  "), "leadingandtrailing")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("no spaces here"), "no spaces here")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("multiple   spaces"), "multiplespaces")\n\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n</answer>\n\n<bash>cat <<EOF > experiments/solution.py\ndef remove_spaces(s):\n    return s.replace(\' \', \'\')\nEOF\ncat <<EOF > experiments/test_solution.py\nimport unittest\nfrom experiments.solution import remove_spaces\n\nclass TestRemoveSpaces(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_remove_spaces(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("hello world"), "helloworld")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("  leading and trailing  "), "leadingandtrailing")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("no spaces here"), "no spaces here")\n        self.assertEqual(remove_spaces("multiple   spaces"), "multiplespaces")\n\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    unittest.main()\nEOF\n</bash>'
