code would be:\n\ndef test_three_equal(a: int, b: int, c: int) -> int:\n    count = {}\n    count[a] = count.get(a, 0) + 1\n    count[b] = count.get(b, 0) + 1\n    count[c] = count.get(c, 0) + 1\n    max_count = max(count.values(), default=0)\n    return max_count if max_count > 1 else 0\n\nWait, but what if all are different? Then count.values() would be three 1s. max is 1, so return 0. Correct.\n\nAnother way: using collections.Counter.\n\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef test_three_equal(a: int, b: int, c: int) -> int:\n    nums = [a, b, c]\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    max_freq = max(freq.values(), default=0)\n    return max_freq if max_freq >= 2 else 0\n\nThis might be more concise. But since the problem says to use long variable names and follow PEP8, maybe the first approach is better, but using Counter is more efficient.\n\nBut the problem doesn't specify whether to use external libraries. Since it's a simple function, using Counter is acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, handle all possible cases with conditionals. Like:\n\nif a == b == c:\n    return 3\nelif a == b or a == c or b == c:\n    return 2\nelse:\n    return 0\n\nThis might be more straightforward and efficient. Let's see.\n\nFor example, in the case where two are same, like a and b, then return 2. If all are different, return 0.\n\nThis approach avoids using extra libraries and is very readable. Which is better? Well, for three variables, checking all combinations is manageable.\n\nSo code:\n\ndef test_three_equal(a: int, b: int, c: int) -> int:\n    if a == b == c:\n        return 3\n    elif a == b or a == c or b == c:\n        return 2\n    else:\n        return 0\n\nThis seems correct. Let's test some examples.\n\nCase 1: 1,1,1 → returns 3. Correct.\n\nCase 2: 1,1,2 → returns 2. Correct.\n\nCase 3: 1,2,3 → returns 0. Correct.\n\nCase 4: 1,2,1 → returns 2. Correct.\n\nThis approach works and is efficient. It also doesn't require any imports.\n\nSo the final code would be this function, saved 