umber?\n\nWait, perhaps I can generate the mask by creating a number where each even bit is 1. For example, for the input number n, find out how many bits it has, then create a mask with 1s in all even positions up to that length.\n\nBut how to do that programmatically.\n\nAlternatively, I can generate a mask that is 0x55555555... (for 32 bits) and then use a bitwise AND with the input number, but that would only work for up to 32 bits. However, in Python, integers can be as long as needed, so maybe the mask can be generated as a number that has 1s in all even positions up to the maximum bit of the input.\n\nWait, but for any n, the mask can be created by shifting 0x5555... appropriately. Alternatively, we can create the mask through a loop.\n\nLet me think of an example. Suppose n is 0b00000000 (0). The mask would be 0x55... but for a 0, it's 0. Not sure.\n\nAnother approach: for each even bit position, set that bit in the mask. So the mask is the sum of 1 << i for all even i from 0 up to the maximum bit in n.\n\nBut what if the number has leading zeros beyond its highest set bit? Like, for example, if n is 4 (binary 100), the highest bit is position 2. So the even positions are 0, 2. The mask would be 1<<0 | 1<<2 = 1 + 4 = 5 (binary 101). XORing n (100) with 5 (101) gives 001, which is 1. But wait, the original n has bits at positions 2, and the mask has 0, 2. So XOR would toggle the even bits. So that works.\n\nSo the algorithm would be:\n\n1. Generate a mask where all even bits (0, 2, 4, etc.) are set up to the highest bit of n.\n\n2. XOR the original number with this mask to toggle those bits.\n\nSo the challenge is to generate the mask correctly.\n\nHow to generate the mask:\n\nLet me think of the mask as having 1s in all even positions. For any given n, the mask should be the same as the mask that would cover all bits up to the highest bit in n. For example, if n is 8 (1000), which is 4 bits. The highest bit is position 3. So the even positions up to 3 are 0,