 steps:\n\nif n is 0:\n\n    return 0\n\nelse:\n\n    max_bit = n.bit_length() -1 \n\n    max_even_bit = max_bit if max_bit % 2 ==0 else max_bit -1\n\n    for i in range(0, max_even_bit +1, 2):\n\n        mask |= 1 << i\n\n    return n ^ mask\n\nWait, but this would loop through all even bits up to the maximum even bit, which may be less than the max bit if max_bit is odd.\n\nBut wait, if the number's highest bit is odd, like 5, then the max even bit is 4. So the mask includes up to 4.\n\nBut that's correct because the problem says to toggle all even positions. The highest bit being odd doesn't affect the mask, as the mask includes even positions up to the highest possible even position.\n\nSo this approach should work.\n\nLet's test with some examples.\n\nExample 1:\n\nn = 5 (binary 101)\n\nbit_length is 3. max_bit = 2 (since 3-1=2). which is even. So max_even_bit is 2.\n\nloop from 0 to 2, step 2:\n\ni=0: 1<<0 =1\n\ni=2: 1<<2=4\n\nmask = 5 (101).\n\nn XOR mask is 101 ^ 101 = 000 (0). Which is correct, because bits at positions 0 and 2 are toggled (original bits are 1 and 0, after toggle they become 0 and 1. Wait, original is 101. So bits:\n\nposition 0: 1\n\nposition 1: 0\n\nposition 2: 1\n\nWait, the original bits in n=5 are positions 0:1, 1:0, 2:1. The even positions (0 and 2) are 1 and 1. Toggling them would make them 0 and 0. So the result should be 000, which is correct. So the code would return 0.\n\nAnother example: n=3 (11). bit_length is 2, max_bit is 1. So max_even_bit is 0.\n\nSo mask is 1<<0 =1. XOR 3 ^1 = 2 (10). Which is correct. The original even bit (position 0) was 1, now 0. The other bits (position 1, odd) is left as is.\n\nAnother example: n=4 (100). bit_length is 3, max_bit 2. mask is 0b101 (5). 4 XOR 5 is 1 (001), which is correct. Bit 0 (was 0, now 1), bit 2 (was 1, now 0).\n\nNow, how to implement this in Python.\n\nThe code can be written as follows:\n\ndef even_bit_toggle_number(n: int) -> int:\n\n    if n == 0:\n\n        return 0\n\n    