nWait, but the list comprehension isn\'t necessary here. The any() function can take a generator expression. So maybe even better to write:\n\nreturn any(a == b, a == c, b == c)\n\nBut in Python, the any() function can take multiple arguments. Wait, no, the any() function takes an iterable. So if you pass multiple arguments, it\'s not correct. Wait, no, the any() function\'s argument is a single iterable. So the correct way is to pass a list or generator that yields the boolean values.\n\nSo, the list [a == b, a == c, b == c] is created, and any() checks if any is True. This approach doesn\'t use any loops, just simple comparisons. So this should meet the requirements.\n\nAnother thought: What about using set operations? For example, converting the sides to a set and checking if the length is less than 3. Because if two sides are equal, the set will have two elements. Wait, but if all three are equal, the set has one element. So the condition would be len(set([a, b, c])) < 3. That would return True for isosceles triangles. But this approach doesn\'t involve any loops either. However, the problem says to use a list comprehension. But in this case, using a set is more concise and doesn\'t involve loops.\n\nBut the rules require using a list comprehension. So maybe the set approach is not acceptable here. The user\'s instructions say to use a list comprehension. So perhaps the solution must use a list comprehension.\n\nBut in the set approach, there\'s no list comprehension. So to comply with the rules, maybe the first approach with the list of booleans is better.\n\nSo the final code would be:\n\ndef check_isosceles(a, b, c):\n    return any([a == b, a == c, b == c])\n\nThis meets all the rules: no loops, uses a list comprehension (the [a==b, ...] part), and checks for any equality. This should work.\n\nWait, but the problem says to use a list comprehension. In Python, the [a == b, a == c, b == c] is a list literal, not a list comprehension. A list comprehension would