ygon.\n\nThe perimeter of a regular polygon is calculated by multiplying the number of sides\nby the length of each side.\n\nFunctions:\n    perimeter_polygon(sides: int, side_length: int) -> int: Calculates the perimeter.\n"""\n\nThen the function definition:\n\ndef perimeter_polygon(sides: int, side_length: int) -> int:\n    """Calculate the perimeter of a regular polygon.\n    \n    Args:\n        sides: The number of sides of the polygon.\n        side_length: The length of each side.\n    \n    Returns:\n        The perimeter of the polygon as an integer.\n    """\n    return sides * side_length\n\nThat should do it. Then, creating the requirements.txt. Since there are no external dependencies, the file can be empty or just have comments. But maybe the user expects it to be created even if empty. So I\'ll create a requirements.txt in the same directory, but it\'s possible that the problem doesn\'t require any dependencies, so that\'s handled.\n\nSo the steps are:\n\n1. Create experiments/solution.py with the function and docstring.\n2. Create experiments/requirements.txt (though maybe the user just wants the code and the requirements.txt if needed).\n\nWait, the user said that the code should be organized into a standard Python package structure. So maybe the solution.py is part of a package. However, since the problem is simple and only requires a single function, the package structure may not be necessary. The user might just want the solution.py in the experiments directory, and the requirements.txt in the same directory.\n\nBut the original problem says to place the solution in experiments/solution.py. So that\'s the main file. Then, the requirements.txt is in the same directory, experiments/requirements.txt. But the user might not have specified that, so perhaps the requirements.txt can be in the root or the same directory. The problem says "all dependencies must be listed in a requirements.txt file," so maybe in the experiments directory, but I\'ll need t