rn False.\n\nSo, how to check that using list comprehensions.\n\nLet\'s think for case 1:\n\nIf s starts with \'+\' or \'-\', then check that all the remaining characters are digits.\n\nFor example, s = \'-123\', then check if all chars after the first are digits.\n\nBut how to do that with a list comprehension.\n\nAlternatively, using filter() to check each character.\n\nAnother approach: for s to be an integer, the string should be all digits, or start with a sign and then all digits.\n\nSo, the function can be written as:\n\ndef check_integer(s):\n    if not s:\n        return False\n    if s[0] in \'+-\':\n        return all(c.isdigit() for c in s[1:]) and len(s) > 1\n    else:\n        return all(c.isdigit() for c in s)\n\nBut this uses a for loop in the all() function. However, all() is a built-in function that uses an iterator, which is allowed.\n\nBut the user\'s instructions say to avoid explicit state management and counters, and to use list comprehensions instead of traditional for loops.\n\nIn this approach, the code is using list comprehensions in the all() function.\n\nWait, the all(c.isdigit() for c in s[1:]) is a generator expression, which is allowed. So this approach would satisfy the user\'s coding standards.\n\nBut the user required using list comprehensions for the task. Let me make sure. The all() function can take a generator, which is created with a generator expression, which is similar to a list comprehension. So perhaps this is acceptable.\n\nSo, the function would be written as such. Let me test some examples.\n\nTest case 1: \'123\' → returns True. Correct.\n\nTest case 2: \'-456\' → returns True. Correct.\n\nTest case 3: \'+789\' → returns True. Correct.\n\nTest case 4: \'12.3\' → returns False. Correct.\n\nTest case 5: \'abc\' → returns False. Correct.\n\nTest case 6: \'\' → returns False. Correct.\n\nTest case 7: \'+\' → returns False (len(s) is 1, so len(s) >1 is false).\n\nTest case 8: \'-0\' → returns True. Correct.\n\nSo, the code