ings. Then create .gitignore in experiments/ with the necessary entries. Ensure the code uses the min function and meets all requirements.\n\nWait, the problem might be testing basic control flow. If the user expects a manual check, like if-else, then using min might be against the intention. But the problem didn\'t specify, so both are correct. To be safe, maybe implement both methods and choose the simplest. Since min is built-in, it\'s efficient and readable. I\'ll proceed with that.\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n# experiments/solution.py\n"""Module containing a function to find the minimum of two integers."""\n\ndef minimum(x: int, y: int) -> int:\n    """Return the smaller of two integers.\n    \n    Args:\n        x: First integer.\n        y: Second integer.\n        \n    Returns:\n        The smaller of x and y.\n    """\n    return min(x, y)\n\n# experiments/.gitignore\n__pycache__\n*.pyc\n*.pyo\n*.pyd\n*.pyi\n*.bak\n*.swp\n*.swo\n*.swn\n*.orig\n*.rej\n*.tmp\n*.log\n*.diff\n*.patch\n*.aux\n*.toc\n*.pdf\n*.DS_Store\n</answer>'
