et\'s see: suppose the original string is s = s_0 s_1 ... s_{n-1}. To have s_j as the last character of the rotated string, the rotation would be to shift by j positions. Wait, no. For example, if j is 0, shifting by 0 positions (no shift) would leave the string as is, so the last character is s_{n-1} (assuming the original string\'s last character is s_{n-1}).\n\nWait, maybe I should think of the rotated string for shift k (number of positions shifted). The rotated string is s_k s_{k+1} ... s_{n-1} s_0 s_1 ... s_{k-1}. The last character of this rotated string is s_{k-1} (if k >0) or s_{n-1} when k=0.\n\nSo for each occurrence of \'1\' in the original string, there\'s exactly one rotation where this \'1\' is at the end of the rotated string. Therefore, the total number of rotations that result in an odd integer is equal to the number of \'1\'s in the original string. Because each \'1\' in the string corresponds to exactly one rotation where it\'s the last digit, making the number odd.\n\nWait, that\'s a huge simplification! So the answer is simply the count of \'1\'s in the binary string. Because each \'1\' contributes exactly one valid rotation. For example, if the binary string is \'101\', there are two \'1\'s, so the answer should be 2. Let\'s check that.\n\nOriginal rotations for \'101\' (length 3):\n\nShift 0: \'101\' → ends with 1 → odd.\n\nShift 1: \'011\' → ends with 1 → odd.\n\nShift 2: \'110\' → ends with 0 → even.\n\nSo total of 2, which matches the number of \'1\'s in the original string (positions 0 and 2).\n\nAnother example: \'110\' (length 3). \'1\'s are at positions 0 and 1.\n\nShift 0: \'110\' → ends with 0 → even.\n\nShift 1: \'101\' → ends with 1 → odd.\n\nShift 2: \'011\' → ends with 1 → odd.\n\nTotal 2, which is the count of \'1\'s in original string. Wait, but original string has two \'1\'s, but here the answer is 2. So that works.\n\nAnother example: \'1000\' (length 4). The \'1\' is at position 0.\n\nShift 0: \'1000\' → ends with 0 → even.\